1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,276
Welcome back to Code Quest.

2
00:00:02,296 --> 00:00:03,640
This is Chapter 4.

3
00:00:03,667 --> 00:00:07,027
Today your programs start a conversation.

4
00:00:07,047 --> 00:00:12,387
They ask a question, listen to the answer,
and calculate with it.

5
00:00:12,433 --> 00:00:15,879
And an AI assistant will promise you 7.50
of change.

6
00:00:15,899 --> 00:00:18,012
Instead, its program will crash.

7
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:21,343
Remember Asha's receipt from Chapter 3?

8
00:00:21,363 --> 00:00:23,664
Neat columns, two decimal places.

9
00:00:23,684 --> 00:00:24,930
Very professional.

10
00:00:24,967 --> 00:00:26,504
But there is a problem.

11
00:00:26,524 --> 00:00:30,567
Every customer gets the same order, two
teas and a sandwich.

12
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:32,222
Omar wants three teas?

13
00:00:32,242 --> 00:00:35,207
Asha has to open the code and change it.

14
00:00:35,227 --> 00:00:38,342
That is not how a real cafe program works.

15
00:00:38,953 --> 00:00:40,790
So here is today's mission.

16
00:00:40,810 --> 00:00:43,953
The program asks the customer three
questions.

17
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,980
Then it prints the receipt for that order,
and only that order.

18
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,640
Different answers, different receipt, and
not one line of code changes.

19
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:54,674
Let's build it, piece by piece.

20
00:00:55,250 --> 00:00:58,470
Ask a question with input, and store the
answer.

21
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:03,090
Explain why every answer is text, even
when it looks like a number.

22
00:01:03,133 --> 00:01:06,743
Convert text into numbers, and numbers
into text.

23
00:01:06,767 --> 00:01:10,387
Write a program that asks, converts and
calculates.

24
00:01:10,433 --> 00:01:13,913
And fix the two errors that conversion
causes most often.

25
00:01:14,483 --> 00:01:17,673
Real programs react to the person who uses
them.

26
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:20,673
A cash machine asks how much money you
want.

27
00:01:20,693 --> 00:01:23,870
A booking site asks how many guests are
coming.

28
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:28,612
Each one asks a question, waits for your
answer, and then uses it.

29
00:01:28,632 --> 00:01:30,117
Today, so will yours.

30
00:01:30,717 --> 00:01:32,095
Here is the big idea.

31
00:01:32,115 --> 00:01:35,757
The function input shows a question, and
then it waits.

32
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:38,674
The user types an answer and presses
Enter.

33
00:01:38,694 --> 00:01:41,770
Then input gives back exactly what they
typed.

34
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:43,915
And here is the important part.

35
00:01:43,935 --> 00:01:45,980
The answer is always a string.

36
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,007
Even when the user types the digit 3, you
get the text 3, not the number.

37
00:01:51,620 --> 00:01:54,368
To calculate with an answer, you convert
it.

38
00:01:54,388 --> 00:01:57,640
Converting means making a new value of
another type.

39
00:01:57,667 --> 00:01:59,585
Int makes a whole number.

40
00:01:59,605 --> 00:02:02,067
The text 3 becomes the number 3.

41
00:02:02,100 --> 00:02:06,540
Float makes a decimal number, for prices
like 4.25.

42
00:02:06,567 --> 00:02:08,550
And str goes the other way.

43
00:02:08,570 --> 00:02:10,628
It turns a number into text.

44
00:02:11,220 --> 00:02:12,438
Think of a waiter.

45
00:02:12,458 --> 00:02:14,845
Every order goes onto a paper slip.

46
00:02:14,865 --> 00:02:17,940
That is input: everything arrives as
writing.

47
00:02:17,967 --> 00:02:20,091
The kitchen cannot add up slips.

48
00:02:20,111 --> 00:02:21,230
It needs numbers.

49
00:02:21,250 --> 00:02:24,447
Conversion reads the slip and writes the
number.

50
00:02:24,467 --> 00:02:27,462
But unlike a person, a conversion cannot
guess.

51
00:02:27,482 --> 00:02:32,339
If the slip says the word two, instead of
the digit, it stops with an error.

52
00:02:32,953 --> 00:02:33,772
Quick check.

53
00:02:33,792 --> 00:02:36,216
The user types 3 and presses Enter.

54
00:02:36,236 --> 00:02:38,032
What does input give back?

55
00:02:49,413 --> 00:02:49,565
B.

56
00:02:49,585 --> 00:02:50,680
The string 3.

57
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:53,167
Input always gives back text.

58
00:02:53,787 --> 00:02:54,801
Take a breather.

59
00:02:54,821 --> 00:02:56,677
Here is what you know so far.

60
00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:59,381
Next, Asha asks her first question.

61
00:03:04,117 --> 00:03:04,640
Step 1.

62
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:07,122
Line 2 asks the customer's name.

63
00:03:07,142 --> 00:03:12,087
The text inside input is the prompt, the
question the user sees.

64
00:03:12,133 --> 00:03:14,667
Notice the space after the question mark.

65
00:03:14,687 --> 00:03:17,593
It keeps the answer from touching the
question.

66
00:03:17,633 --> 00:03:22,993
Lines 3 and 4 greet the customer by name,
with f strings from Chapter 3.

67
00:03:34,347 --> 00:03:36,306
The prompt: What is your name?

68
00:03:36,326 --> 00:03:40,397
Then the program waits on line 2 until the
user presses Enter.

69
00:03:40,433 --> 00:03:46,237
In this course, the output shows the typed
answer after each question, the way your

70
00:03:46,257 --> 00:03:47,219
terminal does.

71
00:03:47,239 --> 00:03:49,043
Here, the user typed Asha.

72
00:03:49,067 --> 00:03:51,833
Then lines 3 and 4 use her answer.

73
00:03:52,417 --> 00:03:52,996
Step 2.

74
00:03:53,016 --> 00:03:56,937
Asha asks how many teas, and the user
types 3.

75
00:03:56,967 --> 00:04:02,197
Line 3 prints the answer and its type,
with the type function from Chapter 2.

76
00:04:02,233 --> 00:04:04,898
Line 4 multiplies the answer by 2.

77
00:04:04,918 --> 00:04:07,740
Remember the big idea about answers.

78
00:04:19,113 --> 00:04:21,033
Line 3 shows class str.

79
00:04:21,053 --> 00:04:22,973
The answer is a string.

80
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,190
So line 4 repeats the text, and prints 33.

81
00:04:26,210 --> 00:04:32,304
That is the same silent bug you met in
Chapter 2: no error message, only a wrong

82
00:04:32,324 --> 00:04:32,839
answer!

83
00:04:33,417 --> 00:04:33,980
Step 3.

84
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,897
The fix is to put input inside int.

85
00:04:36,933 --> 00:04:39,432
Python works from the inside out.

86
00:04:39,452 --> 00:04:42,256
First, input gives back the string 3.

87
00:04:42,276 --> 00:04:45,003
Then int turns it into the number 3.

88
00:04:45,033 --> 00:04:47,318
Line 4 checks the type again.

89
00:04:47,338 --> 00:04:51,450
Line 5 calculates the cost, with two
decimal places.

90
00:05:02,813 --> 00:05:07,283
Line 4 shows class int: a whole number.

91
00:05:07,333 --> 00:05:09,668
So line 5 really calculates.

92
00:05:09,688 --> 00:05:11,182
3 teas cost 10.50.

93
00:05:11,783 --> 00:05:12,248
Step 4.

94
00:05:12,268 --> 00:05:15,500
Remember the price stored as text in
Chapter 3?

95
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:19,030
You were promised a way to turn text into
a number.

96
00:05:19,050 --> 00:05:20,276
Here it is: float.

97
00:05:20,296 --> 00:05:24,843
A price can have a decimal point, like
4.25, so line 2 uses float.

98
00:05:24,867 --> 00:05:28,307
A count is always whole, so line 3 uses
int.

99
00:05:28,333 --> 00:05:29,421
Here is the rule.

100
00:05:29,441 --> 00:05:32,743
Use int for counts, and float for amounts
of money.

101
00:05:32,767 --> 00:05:36,652
Line 4 multiplies, and line 5 prints the
total.

102
00:05:48,013 --> 00:05:50,503
Two questions, one after the other.

103
00:05:50,533 --> 00:05:53,678
Then: 2 cakes cost 8.50.

104
00:05:54,250 --> 00:05:54,780
Step 5.

105
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:59,023
Conversions also work on values you write
in the code.

106
00:05:59,043 --> 00:06:00,830
No questions this time.

107
00:06:00,867 --> 00:06:06,247
Remember the table: int makes a whole
number from text, and str turns a number

108
00:06:06,267 --> 00:06:06,940
into text.

109
00:06:18,313 --> 00:06:23,043
Line 2: the text 12 becomes the number 12,
plus 1 is 13.

110
00:06:23,067 --> 00:06:29,077
Line 7: str turns 4 into text, so plus can
join it to the word teas.

111
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:35,300
Line 3: float turns the text 2.5 into a
number, so times 2 gives 5.0.

112
00:06:35,333 --> 00:06:36,952
Now the surprises.

113
00:06:36,972 --> 00:06:43,233
Line 4: float accepts text without a
decimal point, and adds point 0.

114
00:06:43,267 --> 00:06:45,196
Line 5: int on 3.9 gives 3.

115
00:06:45,216 --> 00:06:47,867
It cuts off the part after the point.

116
00:06:47,887 --> 00:06:49,167
It does not round!

117
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:55,539
And line 6: int ignores spaces at the
start and the end of the text.

118
00:06:56,153 --> 00:06:58,298
Careful with one difference.

119
00:06:58,318 --> 00:07:02,163
Int accepts the number 3.9, and cuts it
down to 3.

120
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:06,561
But the text 3.9, in quotation marks, is
not a whole number.

121
00:07:06,581 --> 00:07:11,380
Int stops with a Value Error, instead of
cutting off the decimals.

122
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:14,709
So for answers with a decimal point, use
float.

123
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:16,270
Quick check.

124
00:07:16,290 --> 00:07:18,372
What does int of 7.8 give?

125
00:07:29,747 --> 00:07:30,152
A, 7.

126
00:07:30,172 --> 00:07:32,366
Int cuts off the decimals.

127
00:07:32,386 --> 00:07:33,899
It does not round.

128
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:35,086
Breather.

129
00:07:35,106 --> 00:07:36,257
Stretch your arms.

130
00:07:36,277 --> 00:07:38,860
You now have every piece of the mission.

131
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:41,475
Let's put them together in Asha's receipt.

132
00:07:46,217 --> 00:07:47,546
Back to the mission.

133
00:07:47,566 --> 00:07:50,043
Lines 2 to 4 ask the three questions.

134
00:07:50,063 --> 00:07:52,607
The two counts are converted with int.

135
00:07:52,633 --> 00:07:55,671
Lines 5 and 6 store the prices, as before.

136
00:07:55,691 --> 00:08:01,349
To keep the program short, the member
discount from Chapter 3 is left out this

137
00:08:01,369 --> 00:08:01,713
time.

138
00:08:01,733 --> 00:08:06,475
Line 8 cleans the name with strip and
title, from Chapter 3.

139
00:08:06,495 --> 00:08:09,253
Lines 9 to 11 calculate the totals.

140
00:08:09,300 --> 00:08:15,611
Lines 12 to 14 build the two labels and
the line, and lines 16 to 22 print the

141
00:08:15,631 --> 00:08:16,260
receipt.

142
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:18,566
Look at lines 2 to 4 once more.

143
00:08:18,586 --> 00:08:20,484
Which answer is different?

144
00:08:31,847 --> 00:08:33,303
The name, on line 2.

145
00:08:33,323 --> 00:08:39,577
It is only cleaned and printed, never used
in a calculation, so it can stay a string.

146
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:40,516
Now Asha runs it.

147
00:08:40,536 --> 00:08:44,480
The customer types omar with extra spaces,
then 3 teas and 2 sandwiches.

148
00:08:44,500 --> 00:08:49,095
And there is Omar's own receipt, with his
name cleaned up.

149
00:08:49,115 --> 00:08:50,130
22.50 to pay.

150
00:08:50,167 --> 00:08:51,294
Mission complete!

151
00:08:51,314 --> 00:08:56,424
But Asha's cafe needs one more thing: how
much change does the customer get?

152
00:08:56,444 --> 00:09:00,204
She builds it with an AI assistant, one
stage at a time.

153
00:09:00,783 --> 00:09:04,673
This chapter's AI skill is debugging with
the exact error.

154
00:09:04,700 --> 00:09:09,850
When a program stops, copy the full error
message and the code into your prompt.

155
00:09:09,900 --> 00:09:11,664
Ask for the cause first.

156
00:09:11,684 --> 00:09:14,265
Only then ask for the smallest fix.

157
00:09:14,285 --> 00:09:17,610
A fix you do not understand will break
again.

158
00:09:17,633 --> 00:09:20,984
And the safety habits from Chapter 1 still
apply.

159
00:09:21,587 --> 00:09:22,661
Stage 1: plan.

160
00:09:22,681 --> 00:09:28,289
Asha describes the change feature, and
asks which type the answer needs.

161
00:09:28,309 --> 00:09:29,227
No code yet.

162
00:09:29,267 --> 00:09:30,990
The AI lists four steps.

163
00:09:31,010 --> 00:09:35,348
Step 2 converts with float, because money
can have decimals.

164
00:09:35,368 --> 00:09:37,818
That matches the rule from step 4.

165
00:09:38,420 --> 00:09:39,507
Stage 2: write.

166
00:09:39,527 --> 00:09:45,705
Asha writes the question line herself, and
asks the AI to explain it from the inside

167
00:09:45,725 --> 00:09:46,000
out.

168
00:09:46,033 --> 00:09:48,916
Input first, then float, then the name.

169
00:09:48,936 --> 00:09:51,892
Exactly the order you learned in step 3.

170
00:09:52,487 --> 00:09:53,893
Stage 3: generate.

171
00:09:53,913 --> 00:09:58,967
Asha asks the AI for the whole program, to
compare it with hers.

172
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:03,311
The AI promises: type 30, and it prints
change 7.50.

173
00:10:03,331 --> 00:10:04,560
It sounds sure.

174
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:08,116
Check every line before you trust it.

175
00:10:19,480 --> 00:10:19,678
No!

176
00:10:19,698 --> 00:10:22,226
Its line 3 has input, but no float.

177
00:10:22,246 --> 00:10:23,827
The answer stays text.

178
00:10:23,847 --> 00:10:26,884
Let's see what happens when Asha tests it.

179
00:10:27,487 --> 00:10:28,517
Stage 4: test.

180
00:10:28,537 --> 00:10:33,847
Asha asks for three test answers, with the
result each one should give.

181
00:10:33,867 --> 00:10:37,317
The AI suggests 30, 22.5, and 25.50.

182
00:10:37,367 --> 00:10:39,414
She tries the first one.

183
00:10:39,434 --> 00:10:44,926
The program shows the question, and then
it stops with an error.

184
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:46,585
Stage 5: debug.

185
00:10:46,605 --> 00:10:48,176
This is today's skill.

186
00:10:48,196 --> 00:10:51,937
Asha pastes the code, her answer and the
full error.

187
00:10:51,957 --> 00:10:53,600
A Type Error on line 4.

188
00:10:53,633 --> 00:10:55,947
She asks for the cause first.

189
00:10:55,967 --> 00:11:02,061
The AI explains: input gives back a
string, and you cannot subtract a number

190
00:11:02,081 --> 00:11:02,866
from text.

191
00:11:02,886 --> 00:11:04,233
The fix is float.

192
00:11:04,267 --> 00:11:06,438
She runs the fix and types 30.

193
00:11:06,458 --> 00:11:07,388
Change: 7.50.

194
00:11:07,408 --> 00:11:09,141
Now the promise is true.

195
00:11:09,753 --> 00:11:11,123
Stage 6: improve.

196
00:11:11,143 --> 00:11:16,763
Asha asks for at most two improvements
that do not change the output.

197
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:19,594
Suggestion 1, a clearer name, helps.

198
00:11:19,614 --> 00:11:24,830
Suggestion 2 says: use int, because
customers pay with whole notes.

199
00:11:24,867 --> 00:11:27,976
Asha uses the AI's own test answer, 25.50.

200
00:11:27,996 --> 00:11:31,106
Int cannot read text with a decimal point.

201
00:11:31,126 --> 00:11:32,000
Value Error!

202
00:11:32,020 --> 00:11:33,714
The AI was wrong again.

203
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:41,130
So Asha keeps suggestion 1 only: the
clearer name, amount paid, with float.

204
00:11:41,167 --> 00:11:43,252
She tests it with 25.50.

205
00:11:43,272 --> 00:11:44,392
Change: 3.00.

206
00:11:44,412 --> 00:11:49,656
The AI helped at every stage, and Asha
checked every answer.

207
00:11:50,253 --> 00:11:51,001
Quick check.

208
00:11:51,021 --> 00:11:53,817
Your program stops after you type an
answer.

209
00:11:53,837 --> 00:11:56,185
What do you paste into the AI prompt?

210
00:12:07,547 --> 00:12:07,663
D.

211
00:12:07,683 --> 00:12:10,457
The code, your answer and the full error.

212
00:12:10,477 --> 00:12:12,434
Then ask for the cause first.

213
00:12:13,053 --> 00:12:13,600
Breather.

214
00:12:13,620 --> 00:12:17,883
You watched Asha catch two AI mistakes,
with what you learned today.

215
00:12:17,933 --> 00:12:21,548
Next, the four mistakes beginners make
most with input.

216
00:12:21,568 --> 00:12:23,794
You have already seen two of them.

217
00:12:28,517 --> 00:12:32,433
Mistake 1 is the same kind of mistake as
the AI's first one.

218
00:12:32,453 --> 00:12:35,517
Line 2 reads the answer, but never
converts it.

219
00:12:35,567 --> 00:12:41,010
So line 3 tries to add 1 to the text 3,
and Python stops with a Type Error.

220
00:12:41,030 --> 00:12:44,580
With plus, the message says can only
concatenate.

221
00:12:44,600 --> 00:12:47,494
With minus, it said unsupported operand.

222
00:12:47,514 --> 00:12:49,097
The cause is the same.

223
00:12:49,133 --> 00:12:53,821
The fix: convert the answer where you read
it, with int around input.

224
00:12:54,420 --> 00:12:57,444
Mistake 2 is the AI's second mistake.

225
00:12:57,464 --> 00:12:58,760
Int for a price.

226
00:12:58,780 --> 00:13:02,380
The user types 4.25, and int cannot read
it.

227
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:05,733
The message says invalid literal with base
10.

228
00:13:05,753 --> 00:13:11,273
A literal is the text Python tried to
read, and base 10 means normal digits.

229
00:13:11,293 --> 00:13:13,387
The fix: use float for money.

230
00:13:13,987 --> 00:13:14,800
Now you try.

231
00:13:14,820 --> 00:13:16,813
The user answers with a word.

232
00:13:16,833 --> 00:13:20,216
Remember the waiter's slip from the idea
section.

233
00:13:31,580 --> 00:13:32,628
A Value Error.

234
00:13:32,648 --> 00:13:35,910
Int cannot guess that the word two means
2.

235
00:13:35,933 --> 00:13:38,356
For now, make the prompt say what to type.

236
00:13:38,376 --> 00:13:42,717
In later chapters, you will learn to check
an answer before you convert it.

237
00:13:43,320 --> 00:13:43,928
One more.

238
00:13:43,948 --> 00:13:48,181
Count the brackets on line 2, like Asha
learned in Chapter 1.

239
00:13:59,547 --> 00:14:00,860
A closing bracket.

240
00:14:00,880 --> 00:14:05,897
Int and input each open one, so the line
needs two closing brackets.

241
00:14:05,933 --> 00:14:10,402
This is a syntax error, so the program
never even asks the question.

242
00:14:11,020 --> 00:14:11,759
Quick check.

243
00:14:11,779 --> 00:14:14,797
Which line reads a price that can have
decimals?

244
00:14:26,180 --> 00:14:26,340
C.

245
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:28,055
Float around input.

246
00:14:28,074 --> 00:14:32,475
Option A keeps text, and option B fails
for 4.25.

247
00:14:33,087 --> 00:14:33,955
Last breather.

248
00:14:33,975 --> 00:14:36,747
These errors will not surprise you any
more.

249
00:14:36,767 --> 00:14:40,081
Two questions to think about on your own
come next.

250
00:14:44,817 --> 00:14:45,239
First.

251
00:14:45,259 --> 00:14:48,482
A customer types 2.0 for the number of
teas.

252
00:14:48,502 --> 00:14:50,104
What happens with int?

253
00:14:50,124 --> 00:14:53,569
And how could float and int together
accept it?

254
00:15:04,947 --> 00:15:07,826
Predict it, then test it after the video.

255
00:15:08,420 --> 00:15:08,919
Second.

256
00:15:08,939 --> 00:15:14,847
Asha wants the program to ask for the
price of tea, instead of fixing it at 3.5.

257
00:15:14,867 --> 00:15:18,256
Which line changes, and which conversion
fits?

258
00:15:29,613 --> 00:15:33,070
Write it, and test it with a price that
has decimals.

259
00:15:33,650 --> 00:15:34,690
Let's recap.

260
00:15:34,733 --> 00:15:38,493
Input shows a prompt, and gives back the
answer as a string.

261
00:15:38,533 --> 00:15:43,133
Int makes a whole number, float a decimal
number, and str makes text.

262
00:15:43,167 --> 00:15:46,467
Python works out int of input from the
inside out.

263
00:15:46,500 --> 00:15:51,310
Int cuts off decimals, and it cannot read
4.25 or the word two.

264
00:15:51,333 --> 00:15:56,731
And with AI: paste the code, your answer
and the full error, and ask for the cause

265
00:15:56,751 --> 00:15:57,127
first.

266
00:15:57,720 --> 00:15:58,814
One last check.

267
00:15:58,834 --> 00:16:02,525
The answer is not converted, and the user
types 2.

268
00:16:02,545 --> 00:16:04,975
What does tea count times 3 show?

269
00:16:16,347 --> 00:16:16,538
B.

270
00:16:16,558 --> 00:16:16,960
222.

271
00:16:16,980 --> 00:16:21,395
The answer is text, so times 3 repeats it.

272
00:16:21,415 --> 00:16:22,874
Convert first!

273
00:16:23,487 --> 00:16:24,789
Mission complete!

274
00:16:24,809 --> 00:16:27,979
You earned 100 XP and the Listener badge.

275
00:16:30,217 --> 00:16:31,773
Now it is your turn.

276
00:16:31,793 --> 00:16:35,557
The practice file has eight tasks, worth
170 XP.

277
00:16:35,600 --> 00:16:38,830
Three warm-up tasks, worth 10 XP each.

278
00:16:38,867 --> 00:16:43,518
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each,
including one receipt line that asks for

279
00:16:43,538 --> 00:16:44,087
its item.

280
00:16:44,133 --> 00:16:46,676
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

281
00:16:46,696 --> 00:16:50,415
In the last one, an AI's ticket program
has two flaws.

282
00:16:50,435 --> 00:16:52,423
Fix them one error at a time.

283
00:16:52,467 --> 00:16:54,060
Predict first, then run.

284
00:16:54,080 --> 00:16:56,951
Try each task before you open the
solution.

285
00:16:57,550 --> 00:17:00,453
Four friends finish their meal at the
Corner Cafe.

286
00:17:00,473 --> 00:17:02,908
They want to add a tip and share the bill.

287
00:17:02,928 --> 00:17:03,960
Who pays how much?

288
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:08,187
In Chapter 5, the first boss level, you
